{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_001","cited_input_aliases":["SRC_001"],"claim":"The target fails to identify the evidence that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance.","grounds":"The problem requires identifying evidence within a single evaluation that separates a true reliability gain from checklist compliance that merely tracks the right answer. The target's mechanism ('Precision-Accuracy Trade-off') argues that non-expert panels reduce variance but may not improve accuracy, and its counterconditions address when the panel might or might not outperform the expert. However, it never specifies the diagnostic evidence within an evaluation that would distinguish genuine reliability from coincidental compliance. It frames the distinction conceptually but omits the operational evidence test the problem demands.","counterexample":["An evaluation could include a sub-sample of items where the checklist's prescribed cues are deliberately decorrelated from the outcome, revealing whether the panel follows the script mechanically (mere compliance) or overrides it using judgment (genuine reliability); the target specifies no such probe."]}